- Last quarter revenue = $2.4M → ≈ $800K/month (recognition vs actual cash collection may differ; important to confirm cash conversion).

Two ways to read your numbers
1. Burn = net burn (72K) → runway ≈ 16.7 months (no other inflows assumed).  
2. Burn = gross outflow (72K) and revenue/cash collections reduce net outflow to 40K → runway = 30 months. In that case revenue’s net monthly cash contribution is ~+$32K.

Ready-to-use finance narrative (short)
- Current position: “As of today, Summit Software holds $1.2M in cash. Our stated monthly cash burn is $72K. If that $72K represents net cash outflow, our available runway is approximately 16.7 months. If instead the $72K is gross spend and our recurring revenue/cash collections offset part of that spend, the implied net outflow consistent with a 30‑month runway is $40K/month — meaning recurring revenue contributes roughly $32K/month of net cash to the business.”
- Drivers & assumptions: “The runway calculation depends heavily on how revenue converts to cash (timing of collections, returns, refunds, and gross margin). Last quarter revenue was $2.4M (~$800K/month), but recognition does not equal collections — verify DSO, deferred revenue, and one-off items. Any deterioration in revenue collection or material one-time spend will reduce runway materially; conversely, improving margins or collections will extend runway.”
