1) Quick arithmetic / immediate flag
- Cash on hand: $1.2M
- Stated burn: $72K/month
- Simple runway = cash / burn = $1,200,000 / $72,000 ≈ 16.7 months

You told me “30 months runway.” Those two statements are inconsistent unless the company is treating runway differently (for example: runway after expected revenue contribution, including committed financing, or using a different definition of “burn”). If the runway truly counts only cash vs net burn, 30 months would imply an effective net burn of $1.2M / 30 = $40K/month.

Please confirm which of the following is true:
- “Burn rate” = net cash burn (cash outflows minus all cash inflows), or is it operating expense run‑rate (excluding product revenue, or excluding non‑cash items)?
- Is the $2.4M last quarter revenue recognized or cash collected? (quarter = which months)
- Is the 30‑month runway an internal metric based on forecasts or includes committed financing or planned revenue?
Once you confirm, I’ll align the narrative and projections.

2) Executive summary (2–3 sentences — use this at the top of the finance deck)
Suggested text:
“Summit Software holds $1.2M in cash as of [date] and delivered $2.4M revenue in the last quarter (annualized ~ $9.6M). Our current net cash burn is $72K/month, implying ~17 months of runway on a cash‑only basis; if we incorporate forecasted revenue growth and planned cost actions, runway stretches to [X – update after you confirm assumptions]. We are focused on sustaining growth while preserving optionality for capital markets decisions over the next 12–18 months.”

3) Key figures and one‑line math to show on slide
- Cash balance: $1,200,000
- Monthly revenue (last quarter / 3): $800,000
- Annualized revenue (simple): $2.4M * 4 = $9.6M
- Net cash burn: $72,000/month
- Runway (cash-only): 1,200,000 / 72,000 ≈ 16.7 months
- If runway target is 30 months, implied net burn = $40K/month (1.2M / 30)

4) Burn trend narrative — how to explain it
- Past trend summary: “Net cash burn has been averaging $72K/month over the last [X] months, down/up from $[prior] as a result of [hiring ramp, customer cancellations, one‑time expenses, R&D investments, subscription seasonality].”
- Drivers: list the main drivers (e.g., payroll and headcount, new product R&D, one‑time legal or M&A costs, sales & marketing spend, channel partner incentives, deferred revenue collections).
- Actionable insight: “Key contributors we can control: (1) slow hiring in non‑critical roles, (2) tighten discretionary spending, (3) restructure vendor contracts, (4) accelerate collections for top 10 customers.”
- If revenue is high relative to burn: highlight profitability of core business and explain any non‑cash charges or working capital timing that cause temporary cash draw.

5) Cash‑flow outlook & scenarios to present (include simple bullets and a chart)
I recommend presenting three scenarios (12–24 months):
- Base case: current burn $72K/month; revenue follows planned forecast (give growth assumption). Show month-by-month cash balance and runway.
- Conservative case: revenue growth slows and burn increases 10–25% (shows earlier capital need).
- Optimistic / Operational improvement: reduce burn to $40K/month (or accelerate collections), demonstrating the 30‑month runway case.

Example quick sensitivity:
- At $72K/mo burn → runway ≈ 16.7 months
- If burn rises 25% → $90K/mo → runway ≈ 13.3 months
- If burn falls to $40K/mo → runway = 30 months

6) KPIs to include on the slide/deck
- Cash balance, runway (months)
- Monthly recurring revenue (MRR) or run‑rate revenue
- Gross margin %
- Net burn (cash outflow)
- CAC, LTV if relevant
- Cash conversion cycle / DSO (days sales outstanding)
- Headcount FTE and cost per FTE trend

7) Recommended immediate actions to include in the finance narrative
- Confirm runway definition and monthly P&L/cashflow transparency
- Perform 12‑month rolling cash forecast with weekly updates
- Prioritize collections: accelerate invoicing or tighten payment terms for largest customers
- Freeze or stage non‑critical hires and discretionary spend
- Evaluate timing for fundraising (if needed) with a target to raise with at least 12–18 months runway under conservative case

8) Suggested slide / paragraph structure for internal materials
- Title: Current Cash Position & Runway
- Slide 1: Executive summary (2–3 lines) + one‑line math
- Slide 2: KPIs and last quarter highlights
- Slide 3: Burn trend chart (last 6–12 months) + drivers
- Slide 4: Cash forecast scenarios (12–24 months)
- Slide 5: Immediate actions & ask (operational items + fundraising ask/timing)

9) Next steps I can do for you (pick one or more)
- I can draft a one‑page CFO memo / 3-slide deck with the narrative above.
- I can build a 12‑month month‑by‑month cash flow model and show the three scenarios if you give:
  - monthly revenue (last 6–12 months) or assumptions for growth,
  - monthly operating expenses (or headcount and avg cost) or the current $72K breakdown,
  - any one‑time upcoming cash items or committed financing.
- I can also produce a short email for leadership summarizing the position and recommended actions.

Which would you like me to prepare next? If you want the model or slides, please confirm the runway definition and provide either monthly P&L or the assumptions above (or say “use these numbers only” and I’ll produce a basic scenario table from the figures you already gave).

