“Cash position: $1.2M. Implied net cash burn: ~$40K/month => ~30 months runway. This extended runway assumes current revenue cash realization and no material one-time expenses. Focus remains on converting pipeline into cash and monitoring quarterly spend. Sensitivity analyses show that, if monthly net burn rises to $72K, runway drops to ~17 months — we therefore recommend preserving contingency actions (hiring pause, discretionary spend cuts) to protect runway.”

B. Supporting talking points / drivers to include
- Definition: state whether “burn” = net cash burn (cash out minus cash in) or gross burn (total cash out).
- Revenue vs cash: clarify whether the $2.4M last quarter is cash collected, billed AR, or bookings; include DSO and deferred revenue trends.
- One-offs: list any non-recurring inflows/outflows (tax refunds, large vendor prepayments, capex).
- Headcount & hiring plan: near-term hiring commitments and associated monthly run-rate delta.
- Customer concentration: % of revenue from top 1–3 customers and collection risk.
- Capital plan: planned fundraising timetable or credit lines and target range.

C. Risks, levers, and recommended actions (what to include in materials)
- Risks: slower collections, unexpected legal or vendor payments, hiring ramp.
- Levers to extend runway: hiring freeze, defer non-critical capex, reduce contractor spend, accelerate collections, negotiate vendor terms.
- Metrics to show: cash balance, net burn (monthly), runway (months), revenue (MRR or monthly), gross margin, DSO, committed ARR/bookings, 13-week cash forecast.
- Scenarios to model: Base, Downside (-25% revenue or +50% burn), Upside (+20% revenue). Show cash balance at 3/6/12/18 months.

D. Suggested exact wording for slide titles and board memo (two-line options)
- “Cash & Runway: $1.2M cash; ~16.7 months runway at $72K/month net burn (or ~30 months if net burn is $40K/month).”
- “Action items: finalize 13-week cash forecast, tighten collection terms, pause discretionary hires, prepare investor update if downside scenario materializes.”

E. Next steps I can do for you
I can build:
- A 12- and 24-month cash-flow projection with monthly granularity and scenario tabs (base / downside / upside).
- A 13-week cash forecast and a one-page board-ready cash/runway slide and talking points.
To do that I need:
